course: dictionary
lesson: 6990
title: Paperwork: papers that must be filled in
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Paperwork is a noun, and it is uncountable, like sand or water. You say «some paperwork» or «a pile of paperwork», not «a paperwork» or «three paperworks». It means forms, letters and reports that must be filled in or kept.
explain: Read the words around it to find its meaning. «Moving means packing everything, doing paperwork and saying goodbye all at once, so it is very stressful.» Packing and saying goodbye are hard jobs, and paperwork is listed as one more job. So paperwork is a job with papers that have to be done.
explain: Often paperwork is seen as boring work that gets in the way of real work. A tired teacher might sit behind «piles of paperwork» and wish she could just teach. Useful phrases are fill in the paperwork, sort out the paperwork and drowning in paperwork.
fact: Paperwork means forms, letters, reports and other papers that must be filled in or kept.
fact: Paperwork is uncountable, so say «some paperwork» or «a pile of paperwork», not «a paperwork».
fact: Paperwork can be for school, for work or for the government, and a permission slip for a school trip is paperwork too.
fact: The boring work of writing, filling in and sorting papers is also called paperwork, and people often feel it gets in the way of real work.
fact: Informally, paperwork can be online, such as a form you fill in on a computer, because it is still an official record.
exercise: «Moving means packing everything, doing paperwork and saying goodbye all at once.» What is paperwork in this sentence? | *Forms and papers that must be filled in | A card to say goodbye to friends | A holiday from school | A kind of packing box
exercise: Which phrase uses paperwork correctly? | three paperworks | a paperwork | *a pile of paperwork | many paperworks
exercise: Mrs Khan says she is drowning in paperwork. What does she mean? | She is swimming in the sea | *She has far too many forms and papers to deal with | She is wearing clothes made of paper | She has lost her shoes in the rain
test: Which of these is paperwork? | A bowl of soup | *A permission slip for a school trip | A football | A bicycle bell
test: Is paperwork a countable noun? | *No, you cannot count it, so you say some paperwork | Only on Sundays | Yes, you can say a paperwork and three paperworks | It depends on the weather
exam: Sam says paperwork is only for grown-ups. Is he right? | No, because paperwork is always a football game | Yes, but only for grown-ups who work in offices | Yes, children never have any papers to fill in | *No, school forms and permission slips are paperwork too
